{
  "_readme": [
    "ACCESSIBILITY AUDIO CUES — 13-accessibility.md §11.",
    "",
    "WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Known gap 34 read: 'the cue points fire and carry their",
    "own gain, but every audio slot in this build is empty, so there is no sound to",
    "play.' That was true and it was the wrong conclusion. §11 does not specify",
    "audio *files* — it specifies a **cue character** for each of its 34 rows:",
    "'two-note rising chime', 'single low tone', 'discrete ticks, rising pitch',",
    "'hollow descending tone, pitch drops per stack'. Those are descriptions of a",
    "waveform, and a waveform is something an AudioContext can make out of nothing.",
    "So the cues are synthesised. No files, no downloads, no licence, and no empty",
    "slot pretending to be a sound.",
    "",
    "THE BINDING RULE, from §11: 'every cue has a visual twin. No cue is the only",
    "notification of anything.' Every row here names the twin it accompanies, and",
    "`checkCueData` fails on a cue that names none.",
    "",
    "CAPTIONS. §11 marks some rows captioned; those carry `caption`, which the",
    "sound-captions setting prints on screen. A captioned row with no caption text",
    "is a caption setting that shows nothing, so that is a load error too.",
    "",
    "DISTINCTNESS. A cue nobody can tell from another cue is worse than silence,",
    "because it reads as the game having got it wrong. Two cues may not share a",
    "tone sequence, and the test asserts it — the same mechanical guardrail §7.2's",
    "palette check applies to colour.",
    "",
    "WAVES. sine / triangle / square / sawtooth are oscillators; 'noise' is a",
    "filtered burst, which is what 'paper slide', 'glass tap' and 'crumple' are.",
    "`hz` on a noise tone sets the filter's centre rather than a pitch."
  ],

